<h1>Experiments</h1>
<p>
  This page demonstrates a handful of HTML5 tags that don't appear elsewhere on the site.
  Each block below is a minimal example of a specific tag in use.
</p>

<section>
  <h2>Audio</h2>
  <p>A classic throwback sound:</p>
  <audio controls>
    <source src="/media/classicalmusic1.mp3" type="audio/mpeg">
    Your browser does not support the audio element.
  </audio>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Video</h2>
  <p>A serval cat in nature:</p>
  <video controls width="400">
    <source src="/media/sleepingServal.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    Your browser does not support the video element.
  </video>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Template</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> element holds inert content that exists in the HTML
    but does not render on the page. Part 2's web component uses this same technique
    to build its markup safely.
  </p>
  <template id="reusable-card">
    <article>
      <h3>Reusable card</h3>
      <p>This content lives inside a template tag.</p>
    </article>
  </template>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>MathML</h2>
  <p>Einstein's mass-energy equivalence, rendered with MathML:</p>

  <math display="block">
    <mi>E</mi>
    <mo>=</mo>
    <mi>m</mi>
    <msup>
      <mi>c</mi>
      <mn>2</mn>
    </msup>
  </math>

  <h3>Why MathML wasn't usable until recently</h3>
  <p>
    Even though MathML has technically been part of the HTML spec for a long time, developers
    basically couldn't use it until recently because browser support was inconsistent.
    <a href="https://caniuse.com/mathml">Caniuse</a> shows that Firefox supported MathML for years,
    while Chrome, Edge, and Safari either didn't support it at all or supported only parts of it.
    An equation might look correct in Firefox and break in Chrome, so most developers reached for
    JavaScript libraries like MathJax instead. Chrome didn't add real native support until version
    109 in early 2023, which is when it became something you could use across all major browsers
    without a fallback.
  </p>
</section>
